CVE-2026-65660 is a Microsoft SharePoint code injection vulnerability caused by improper control of code generation (CWE-94). An authenticated attacker with low privileges can exploit it remotely over the network to execute code on a vulnerable SharePoint server. Microsoft rates it CVSS 8.8 (High).
Impact
•	Remote code execution (RCE) on SharePoint servers.
•	Requires an authorized/authenticated attacker.
•	Can affect confidentiality, integrity, and availability.
Affected Products
The following versions are affected if they are older than the patched versions below: [NVD-CVE-2026-65660]
•	Microsoft SharePoint Enterprise Server 2016 
o	Vulnerable: versions earlier than 16.0.5565.1001. [NVD-CVE-2026-65660]
•	Microsoft SharePoint Server 2019 
o	Vulnerable: versions earlier than 16.0.10417.20198. [NVD-CVE-2026-65660]
•	Microsoft SharePoint Server Subscription Edition 
o	Vulnerable: versions earlier than 16.0.19725.20522. [NVD-CVE-2026-65660]
Patch / Remediation
Microsoft's security update is the primary fix. Upgrade to at least the following builds: [NVD-CVE-2026-65660]
Product	Fixed Version
SharePoint Enterprise Server 2016	16.0.5565.1001 or later
SharePoint Server 2019	16.0.10417.20198 or later
SharePoint Server Subscription Edition	16.0.19725.20522 or later

 
From a Lockheed Martin Cyber Kill Chain perspective, CVE-2026-65660 represents a particularly dangerous vulnerability because it enables remote code execution (RCE) in Microsoft SharePoint after an attacker obtains a valid account. The vulnerability is classified as CWE-94 (Code Injection) and allows an authorized attacker to execute code over the network. Microsoft scores it CVSS 8.8 (High). 
Mapping CVE-2026-65660 to the cyber kill chain
1. Reconnaissance
The attacker identifies:
Internet-facing SharePoint deployments
SharePoint version/build information
Usernames and authentication mechanisms
Potential low-privilege accounts
Because the vulnerability requires an authorized attacker, reconnaissance is focused not only on the server but also on obtaining credentials
2. Weaponization
The attacker develops or acquires:
A SharePoint-specific exploit
Malicious code payloads
Web shells
Command execution scripts
Persistence mechanisms
Since the flaw is a code injection vulnerability, the weaponized component is the code that will be injected into the vulnerable SharePoint functionality. The NVD explicitly describes the issue as improper control of code generation leading to code execution. [NVD-CVE-2026-65660]
3. Delivery
Unlike phishing-based attacks, delivery here typically occurs through:
Direct HTTP/HTTPS requests to SharePoint
Authenticated SharePoint sessions
Stolen or compromised credentials
The exploit is delivered through the application's exposed network interfaces.
Defensive controls
MFA
Conditional Access
VPN restrictions
Internet exposure reduction
Web Application Firewall logging
4. Exploitation
This is the stage where CVE-2026-65660 directly applies.
The attacker:
Authenticates with a low-privilege account.
Sends crafted requests.
Triggers code generation weaknesses.
Achieves execution of attacker-controlled commands.
The CVSS vector indicates:
AV:N/AC:L/PR:L/UI:N
Meaning:
Network exploitable
Low attack complexity
Low privileges required
No user interaction required
These properties make exploitation highly attractive to threat actors. 
5. Installation
After successful code execution, attackers commonly install:
Web shells
Backdoors
Scheduled tasks
Additional malware
Credential theft tools
In SharePoint environments this frequently means placing a web shell inside web-accessible directories to provide persistent access.
Example kill-chain progression:
Authenticated User
        ↓
Exploit CVE-2026-65660
        ↓
Execute PowerShell
        ↓
Drop Web Shell
        ↓
Persistent Access
6. Command and Control (C2)
Once persistence is established, the compromised SharePoint server becomes a foothold.
The attacker may:
Connect to external C2 infrastructure
Tunnel traffic over HTTPS
Use PowerShell-based beacons
Establish reverse shells
Since SharePoint servers often reside in trusted network segments, successful compromise can provide substantial lateral movement opportunities.
